	and projection constructors need of an element.

	`f32` and `f64` meet it through this package. A user type meets it with
	inherent methods, and, when it is declared in `package main` or is
	otherwise private, with `@(public)` operators and conversion, since those
	requirements are checked here:

	    Dual :: struct { value, derivative: f64 }

	    impl Dual {
	        @(public) from_f64 :: hook(convert) proc(value: f64) -> Dual { ... }
	        @(public) add :: operator(+) proc(a, b: Dual) -> Dual { ... }
	        // ... -, *, /, unary -, ==, <
	        sqrt :: proc(self) -> Dual { ... }
	        // ... and the other slots
	    }

	`is_normal` is true for a finite value that is not zero and has full
	precision; it is what the degenerate input checks test.
*/
Real :: interface($T: type) {
	interfaces.Numeric(T);
	interfaces.Ordered(T);
